WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.680
Americans love sausage and bacon.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.610
We consider it almost a
civil right to have access

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:10.670
to abundant low-cost protein supplies.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:11.890
Some of the same factors

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:14.390
that make the meat
supply chain so efficient

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:17.930
and profitable are also helping
fuel devastating outbreaks

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.980
of a novel coronavirus.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.300
And according to the US' largest supplier,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.427
that supply chain is breaking.

00:00:24.427 --> 00:00:27.830
More meat processing plants
in the Midwest are closing,

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.200
and that could impact our food supply.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.980
There are growing concerns
about food shortages.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.570
So far, over 10,000
workers have been infected

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.740
or exposed to COVID-19,
and at least 30 have died.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.640
Our members are getting
sick, and they're dying.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.170
Worker safety and health

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.670
in this pandemic is really in crisis.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:48.713
A lot of us were getting scared,

00:00:48.713 --> 00:00:51.092
knowing that if we
didn't show up for work,

00:00:51.092 --> 00:00:52.070
we might not have a job.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.150
An executive order
signed by President Trump

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:59.600
declares meat processing plants
as critical infrastructure.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.610
If the administration wants
to protect the food chain,

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:07.660
what they really need to do
is to protect the health,

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.430
the safety of the
workers in that industry.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:14.290
If American consumers are to continue

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.650
to enjoy cheap and plentiful meat,

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.800
processing companies will have
to navigate a fragile balance

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.553
of safety and supply.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.360
Before we talk about what's happening

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.970
inside these processing plants,

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.520
it's important to understand their place

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.023
in the supply chain for
meat in the United States.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.390
Farmers raise livestock

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.230
to a certain weight and size measurement

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:37.920
so that they can sell
them to meat processors.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.520
And at these processing plants,

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.690
factory workers will
often kill the animal,

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:43.460
break it down into different cuts,

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.760
and package it to the end product.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.520
That is then transportable
for your local grocery store

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.070
and other food service
areas like restaurants

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.560
or catering companies.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.400
And it's the efficiency and
scale of this meat production

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.640
in the United States that
allow prices to be kept so low.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.260
So when some processing centers
had to slow down production,

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.814
and in a few cases shut down entirely,

00:02:03.814 --> 00:02:05.740
All of a sudden, we have a big bottleneck

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.820
of animals with nowhere to go.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.503
In some instances, we've seen farmers

00:02:09.503 --> 00:02:12.240
that have had to start calling animals

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.010
because there's just
nowhere to send their pigs.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.480
We'll end up having to
euthanize either baby pigs

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.810
or even market hogs on that end

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.520
'cause there's just simply
nowhere to go with them.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.980
And that bottleneck in
these processing centers

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.060
means that grocery stores

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.820
in some areas may see a
reduced amount of supply.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.970
At the same time, these
stores are seeing an increase

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.840
in demand with restaurants closing

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.950
and people eating in more often.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.880
It instills a lot of fear in consumers

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:41.680
and ultimately in voters
to have that right

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:44.940
to affordable low-cost meat threatened.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.450
But ramping production back up

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:47.900
in these processing plants

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.700
and ensuring safe conditions
for workers is challenging

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.610
for the same reasons that
normally make them so efficient,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.280
their size and the speed
at which they operate.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.730
Let's start with their size.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.100
What we've seen, the coronavirus pandemic,

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.070
is kind of highlight this
industry concentration.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:09.070
A handful of very, very large
companies control the bulk

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.850
of Americans' meat supply.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:14.080
So, you have Tyson, JBS, and Cargill

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.270
that account for about
2/3 of America's beef.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:20.910
And the large bulk of it is
produced in a few dozen plants.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:24.860
From the late '60s until now,
we've seen about a 70% decline

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:28.080
in just the number of
slaughterhouses that exist.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.950
This industry consolidation has meant

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:31.640
that while there are fewer plants,

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.410
each plant processes far
more meat than ever before.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.040
And even a single plant
shutdown can have an impact

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.500
on the overall supply.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.420
When just one Smithfield plant
in South Dakota shut down,

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.850
it reduced pork supply in
the country by four to 5%.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.370
The other side of their size

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.140
is just how many people
work in these plants.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.890
Some of these plants are very large.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:53.020
There's thousands of employees.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.080
It's just the nature of this sort of work.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.760
A lot of industries
obviously rely on automation.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.890
And in pork and beef slaughterhouses,

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:02.200
it's not really like that.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.670
You still have a lot of hands-on labor.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:07.050
It can be up to 2,000 workers on a shift.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:08.400
They crowd into break rooms.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:09.820
They crowd into locker rooms.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.670
They all punch-in around the same time

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:14.060
using the same clocks.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.160
It's loud in these facilities,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.470
so workers have to get
really close together

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:20.680
to hear each other.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.410
These plants have workers

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:23.890
working shoulder-to-shoulder

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.340
in every single department.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.740
And that brings us to the speed

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.200
of these facilities.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.390
By now you've probably
noticed just how closely some

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:33.180
of these workers are to one another.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.600
The reason for that is largely

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.030
to allow the production
line to move faster.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:39.390
The more hands along
the line allows the belt

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.690
to move at a faster rate

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:43.350
and therefore deliver more
meat at the end of the day.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.480
And it's really physically demanding work,

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.780
and the pace of the line goes so fast

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.820
that workers rarely have
time to go to the bathroom

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:54.260
because their job is to get the meat out.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:58.200
In order to put space between the workers,

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.090
they have to slow down the line speeds.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.800
In order to create a safer environment

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.700
and manage these multiple threats,

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.820
they have to slow down the line speeds.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.660
With a potential for reduced line speeds

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.690
and large factories still
slowly coming back online,

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:14.930
returning processing to full capacity

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:17.170
may be a difficult task for sometime.

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:19.040
And between growing pressure from farmers

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.990
who have nowhere to take their livestock

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:24.400
and executives warning of
a looming supply shortage,

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.960
the government stepped in.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:28.700
Facing fears of a nation
wide meat shortage

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:30.640
President Trump signed
an executive order today

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:34.450
to keep processing plants
open, despite safety concerns.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:38.100
We just worked with the meat processors.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.220
They're thrilled, and that
whole bottleneck is broken up.

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:43.170
But labor groups fear

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.110
that ramping plants up too quickly

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.560
can mean workers will
continue to be put at risk.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.790
Well, companies have been very clear

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:53.000
that they have been taking
steps to protect workers.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.880
So, they've done things
like increase sanitation,

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:57.580
space out breaks.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.310
They're offering PPE,
protective gear like masks.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:04.380
We remain committed to our
employees during this time,

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.470
and we're implementing many changes

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.170
to assist our team members.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.710
We have enhanced the
cleaning and disinfection,

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:13.180
our frequently-touched
objects and surfaces.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.340
And we're continuing to
stress the importance

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:16.470
of personal hygiene.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.100
Measuring temperatures as
they come through the door,

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.920
face coverings, staggered
breaks, expanded room,

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:24.530
social distancing,

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.510
all of these are designed to
help keep our workers safe.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:28.720
Keeping our workers safe

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:30.600
is what will keep our plants running.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.360
But currently companies face

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:35.110
no outside enforcement
of these safety measures.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:37.360
And the Centers for Disease
Control and Prevention,

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.480
instead of issuing very clear guidance

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:45.140
and recommendations on
what employers need to do

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:46.720
to protect workers,

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.270
they watered it down so it says
if possible, consider this,

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:52.560
in almost every sentence

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.560
so that it doesn't really
give employers any mandates.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:02.460
So, there's just a lot going on

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:06.000
that shields companies
from responsibility,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.160
pressures them to keep production up,

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.560
and disempowers workers.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:14.740
You know, I am a meat eater.

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:19.620
I love bacon and breakfast
sausage and all of it.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:23.090
We have to, we bacon-loving consumers

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.740
have to adapt to the near-term reality

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.670
of some lower volumes of
meat, higher costs of meat.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:33.360
And we have to be willing
as consumers to accept that.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.360
We're not facing severe
food shortages right now.

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:42.290
What could drive severe
food shortages longterm

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:44.390
is if we're reckless,

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:47.180
if the companies and the management

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.890
of these companies is
reckless about the health

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.683
and wellbeing of workers.

